<h2 data-section-id="uhsvtf" data-start="46" data-end="83">Skills to lean on for this project</h2>
<p data-start="85" data-end="152">These are the four I’d actually use as the core stack for this job:</p>
<ol data-start="154" data-end="951">
<li data-section-id="1w6tp9d" data-start="154" data-end="348">
<p data-start="157" data-end="348"><strong data-start="157" data-end="181">figma-internal-scout</strong><br data-start="181" data-end="184">
Use for inspecting your existing Figma files and turning them into a concrete inventory of sections, reusable patterns, variants, spacing logic, and token usage.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="11hbk7r" data-start="350" data-end="559">
<p data-start="353" data-end="559"><strong data-start="353" data-end="379">figma-to-code-contract</strong><br data-start="379" data-end="382">
Use for turning the winning Figma sections into implementation-ready component contracts: props, states, responsive behavior, slot structure, and “do not break” layout rules.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="11rmc6z" data-start="561" data-end="783">
<p data-start="564" data-end="783"><strong data-start="564" data-end="590">figma-knowledge-weaver</strong><br data-start="590" data-end="593">
Use for keeping a durable design memory: which homepage patterns won, what your brand rules are, what the proof rail is allowed to say, what project-card fields belong on home vs gallery.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="14unka2" data-start="785" data-end="951">
<p data-start="788" data-end="951"><strong data-start="788" data-end="812">sequential-workbench</strong><br data-start="812" data-end="815">
Use for the project-level discipline: sequence the homepage upgrade, repo separation plan, and later portal cleanup without drifting.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-section-id="1a4lx65" data-start="953" data-end="972">Secondary skill</h3>
<ol start="5" data-start="973" data-end="1314">
<li data-section-id="2ogiqs" data-start="973" data-end="1314">
<p data-start="976" data-end="1189"><strong data-start="976" data-end="1001">figma-community-miner</strong><br data-start="1001" data-end="1004">
Use this selectively. It should inform <strong data-start="1046" data-end="1077">interaction and composition</strong>, not drive the visual language. Your brand already has a real point of view. Community patterns are useful for:</p>
<ul data-start="1193" data-end="1314">
<li data-section-id="lxn1aa" data-start="1193" data-end="1217">
<p data-start="1195" data-end="1217">proof rail composition</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="egx27o" data-start="1221" data-end="1251">
<p data-start="1223" data-end="1251">project evidence/map layouts</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="1wov5re" data-start="1255" data-end="1278">
<p data-start="1257" data-end="1278">testimonial mechanics</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id="r61hu2" data-start="1282" data-end="1314">
<p data-start="1284" data-end="1314">intake/estimate flow structure</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="1316" data-end="1369">They are <strong data-start="1325" data-end="1332">not</strong> the source of truth for visual tone.</p>
